some  may describe it as a constitutional  earthquake as we feel the reverberations  of what has happened  we now automatically have a new king  and it's literally been confirmed in the  last few moments that the new king will  be known as king charles iii  now  at home you might be thinking well of  course he would be but actually when it  comes to a change  in reign the new sovereign is able to  choose a different name and of course  we've seen that throughout history so  instead of being king charles he could  have chosen any of his other names which  are philip arthur or george however we  now know that he will be king charles  iii he also takes on other titles so  head of the commonwealth  commander-in-chief of the armed forces  supreme governor of the church of  england  so we have the constitutional change we  have the change in  head of state  but i think what we're also going to see  and i think you're already probably  seeing there at buckingham palace you  will have a greater sense of this  is  a sense that people want to come  together  a collective sense of shock  and mourning it was after all only  two days ago that we saw her still  carrying out her constitutional duties  and as liz trust said in her speech  there are going to be difficult days  ahead where  communities will come together  just as we have done right throughout  her reign to mark those milestone  moments to  pay our respects to  what was a life devoted to service  indeed rihanna thank you for that and i  i would echo  those thoughts in  terms of what people here  are coming to witness and to experience  there's  a huge sense  obviously and clearly of the the end of  an era but also  a little bit of trepidation about what  lies ahead we know that we are in  very difficult times both nationally and  internationally and at this very moment  the rock  as these trusts referred to at the very  spirits of great britain the rock upon  which modern britain was built  has passed  and in just one  short week we have  had the changing of a prime minister now  the  passing of our longest-serving  head of state the rock upon which  britain was built um alastair bruce our  rule commentator  joins me now and um  alastair just on that confirmation now  that um  the king will be king charles  iii and um  camilla queen consort  yes it's a  moment of continuity and a moment of  great sadness  i think for  a nation recognizing that the queen is  now dead  it must be a great shot for the new king  that instantaneously while he  fathoms the loss of his mother  there will be red boxes to be dealt with  there will be the work that the queen  didn't quite finish yesterday  and he must now  continue the function of government as  head of state  immediately  and that process and continuity of  playing the role as monarch  is one where everybody now will look to  king charles iii for decisions on  everything  all the  needs and requirements of the days ahead  will be placed for him to make decisions  on and it is a very busy changing and  the queen referred to it herself when  her father died  back in 1952 that suddenly everybody  looked to her  and she didn't necessarily feel prepared  for it and yet  in her 71st year as monarch she became  so  comfortably adept and with that glinting  eye  when she was waiting for the arrival of  her  15th prime minister in the drawing room  of balmoral castle she wanted to make  photographer waiting  feel at ease and made a charming comment  to him  and i think that's always been the  nature of how she's done this role  is she's  always managed to put herself last  and to approach it with great humility  and she's given that torch  now to the new king who must take on so  much and yet at the same time at this  moment with  his brothers and sister and other  members of the family are recognizing  the loss of their second parent and  somebody sent a message to me now that  you know  lots of people feel they've lost a  parent  and that is the nature of monarchy it  reminds us because we get to know  the monarch almost as though she's a  personal friend  and when she goes  it's like losing  somebody and it echoes a loss in one's  own family and i think that those people  gathering now outside buckingham palace  each of them with their own personal  thoughts  they've come for myriad reasons  and a head of state has to encompass  myriad people with myriad thoughts  and elizabeth ii did that and now the  new king must take on that  responsibility

